ses L135:     * cite125†Latest Releases L136:     * cite126†Major Economic Indicators L137:     * cite127†Schedules for news Releases L138:     * cite128†By Month L139:     * cite129†By News Release L140:     * cite130†Current Year L141:     * cite131†Prior Years L142:     * cite132†Archived News Releases L143:     * cite133†Employment & Unemployment L144:     * cite133†Monthly L145:     * cite133†Quarterly, annual, and other L146:     * cite134†Inflation & Prices L147:     * cite135†Pay & Benefits & Workplace Injuries L148:     * cite136†Productivity & Technology L149:     * cite137†Employment Projections L150:     * cite138†Regional News Releases L151:   * cite139†Classroom L152: \nL153:     * cite139†K-12 Student & Teacher Resources L154:     * cite140†Games & Quizzes L155:     * cite141†Student's Desk L156:     * cite142†Teacher's Desk L157:     * cite143†Questions & Answers L158: \nL159: cite124†Economic News Release L160: \nL161: cite144†PRINT:†data.bls.gov L162: \nL163: cite145†CPS L164: \nL165: Labor Force Statistics from the Current Population Survey\nL166: \nL167:   * cite52†CPS Homepage L168:   * cite146†CPS Overview L169:   * cite147†CPS FAQ L170:   * cite148†Contact CPS L171: \nL172: [Button: Close]\nL173: \nL174: cite149†CES L175: \nL176: Current Employment Statistics - CES (National)\nL177:   * cite43†CES Homepage L178:   * cite150†CES Overview L179:   * cite151†CES FAQs L180:   * cite152†Contact CES L181: \nL182: [Button: Close]\nL183: # Employment Situation News Release\nL184: \nL185:     Transmission of material in this news release is embargoed until                       USDL-26-1549\nL186:     8:30 a.m. (ET) Friday, October 2, 2026\nL187: \nL188:     Technical information:\nL189:      Household data:      (202) 691-6378  *  cpsinfo@bls.gov  *  www.bls.gov/cps\nL190:      Establishment data:  (202) 691-6555  *  cesinfo@bls.gov  *  www.bls.gov/ces\nL191: \nL192:     Media contact:        (202) 691-5902  *  PressOffice@bls.gov\nL193:                                 THE EMPLOYMENT SITUATION - SEPTEMBER 2026\nL194: \nL195: \nL196:     Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in\nL197:     September, the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported today. Employment in all major industries\nL198:     changed little over the month.\nL199: \nL200:     This news release presents statistics from two monthly surveys. The household survey measures labor\nL201:     force status, including unemployment, by demographic characteristics. The establishment survey\nL202:     measures nonfarm employment, hours, and earnings by industry. For more information about the\nL203:     concepts and statistical methodology used in these two surveys, see the Technical Note.\nL204: \nL205:     Household Survey Data\nL206: \nL207:     Both the unemployment rate, at 4.2 percent, and the number of unemployed people, at 7.1 million,\nL208:     changed little in September. The unemployment rate has remained in a narrow range of 4.1 percent to\nL209:     4.3 percent since March. (See table A-1.)\nL210: \nL211:     Among the major worker groups, the unemployment rate for people who are Black (7.0 percent)\nL212:     increased in September. The jobless rates for adult men (3.9 percent), adult women (3.6 percent),\nL213:     teenagers (14.5 percent), and people who are White (3.6 percent), Asian (2.9 percent), or Hispanic\nL214:     (4.7 percent) showed little change over the month. (See tables A-1, A-2, and A-3.)\nL215: \nL216:     The number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) was essentially unchanged\nL217:     at 1.9 million in September. The long-term unemployed accounted for 27.1 percent of all unemployed\nL218:     people. (See table A-12.)\nL219: \nL220:     Both the labor force participation rate, at 61.8 percent, and the employment-population ratio, at\nL221:     59.2 percent, changed little in September. These measures showed little net change since January.\nL222:     (See table A-1.)\nL223: \nL224:     The number of people employed part time for economic reasons changed little at 4.5 million in\nL225:     September. These individuals would have preferred full-time employment but were working part time\nL226:     because their hours had been reduced or they were unable to find full-time jobs. (See table A-8.)\nL227:     In September, the number of people not in the labor force who currently want a job changed little\nL228:     at 5.8 million. These individuals were not counted as unemployed because they were not actively\nL229:     looking for work during the 4 weeks preceding the survey or were unavailable to take a job. (See\nL230:     table A-1.)\nL231: \nL232:     Among those not in the labor force who wanted a job, the number of people marginally attached to\nL233:     the labor force decreased by 236,000 to 1.5 million in September. These individuals wanted and were\nL234:     available for work and had looked for a job sometime in the prior 12 months but had not looked for\nL235:     work in the 4 weeks preceding the survey. The number of discouraged workers, a subset of the\nL236:     marginally attached who believed that no jobs were available for them, changed little over the\nL237:     month at 414,000. (See Summary table A.)\nL238: \nL239:     Establishment Survey Data\nL240:     Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000), following an average\nL241:     monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months. Employment in all major industries changed little\nL242:     over the month. (See table B-1.)\nL243: \nL244:     Health care employment continued its upward trend in September (+17,000), but at a slower pace than\nL245:     the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+33,000). In September, employment continued to\nL246:     trend up in ambulatory health care services (+13,000) and in hospitals (+12,000), while nursing and\nL247:     residential care facilities lost jobs (-9,000).\nL248: \nL249:     Employment in construction changed little in September (+11,000). The industry had added an average\nL250:     of 10,000 jobs per month over the prior 12 months. In September, employment in nonresidential\nL251:     specialty trade contractors continued to trend up (+12,000).\nL252:     Manufacturing employment was little changed in September (+9,000) but is up by 72,000 since a\nL253:     recent low in December 2025. Over the month, employment increased in plastics and rubber products\nL254:     manufacturing (+5,000) and in machinery manufacturing (+5,000).\nL255: \nL256:     In September, financial activities employment was little changed (-7,000). Employment in financial\nL257:     activities is down by 129,000 since a recent peak in May 2025, with most of the job loss in\nL258:     insurance carriers and related activities (-90,000).\nL259: \nL260:     Employment also showed little change over the month in other major industries, including mining,\nL261:     quarrying, and oil and gas extraction; wholesale trade; retail trade; transportation and\nL262:     warehousing; information; professional and business services; social assistance; leisure and\nL263:     hospitality; other services; and government.\nL264:     In September, average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls edged up by 5\nL265:     cents, or 0.1 percent, to $37.81. Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased\nL266:     by 3.0 percent. In September, average hourly earnings of private-sector production and\nL267:     nonsupervisory employees rose by 7 cents, or 0.2 percent, to $32.60. (See tables B-3 and B-8.)\nL268: \nL269:     The average workweek for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls remained at 34.4 hours in\nL270:     September. In manufacturing, the average workweek was unchanged at 40.6 hours, and overtime held at\nL271:     3.0 hours. The average workweek for production and nonsupervisory employees on private nonfarm\nL272:     payrolls remained at 33.8 hours. (See tables B-2 and B-7.)\nL273: \nL274:     The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for July was revised down by 31,000, from +21,000 to\nL275:     -10,000, and the change for August was revised down by 29,000, from +162,000 to +133,000. With\nL276:     these revisions, employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported.\nL277:     (Monthly revisions result from additional reports received from businesses and government agencies\nL278:     since the last published estimates and from the recalculation of seasonal factors.)\nL279: \nL280:     _____________\nL281:     The Employment Situation for October 2026 is scheduled to be published on Friday, November 6, 2026,\nL282:     at 8:30 a.m. (ET).\nL283: HOUSEHOLD DATA\nL284: Summary table A. Household data, seasonally adjusted [Numbers in thousands]\nL285: Category  | Sept.\nL286: 2025  | July\nL287: 2026  | Aug.\nL288: 2026  | Sept.\nL289: 2026  | Change from:\nL290: Aug.\nL291: 2026-\nL292: Sept.\nL293: 2026\nL294: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL295: Employment status  |\nL296: Civilian noninstitutional population(cite153†1 )  | 274,226  | 275,282  | 275,415  | 275,554  | 139\nL297: Civilian labor force  | 171,261  | 169,094  | 169,777  | 170,262  | 485\nL298: Participation rate  | 62.5  | 61.4  | 61.6  | 61.8  | 0.2\nL299: Employed  | 163,656  | 162,177  | 162,746  | 163,152  | 406\nL300: Employment-population ratio  | 59.7  | 58.9  | 59.1  | 59.2  | 0.1\nL301: Unemployed  | 7,605  | 6,916  | 7,031  | 7,109  | 78\nL302: Unemployment rate  | 4.4  | 4.1  | 4.1  | 4.2  | 0.1\nL303: Not in labor force  | 102,964  | 106,189  | 105,638  | 105,292  | -346\nL304: Unemployment rates  |\nL305: Total, 16 years and over  | 4.4  | 4.1  | 4.1  | 4.2  | 0.1\nL306: Adult men (20 years and over)  | 4.1  | 3.9  | 4.0  | 3.9  | -0.1\nL307: Ad