the industries with unchanged employment, where 50 percent indicates an equal balance between industries with increasing and decreasing employment.\nL400: (p) Preliminary\nL401: NOTE: Data have been revised to reflect March 2025 benchmark levels and updated seasonal adjustment factors.\nL402: \nL403:     Frequently Asked Questions about Employment and Unemployment Estimates\nL404: \nL405:     1. Why are there two monthly measures of employment?\nL406: \nL407:        The household survey and establishment survey both produce sample-based estimates\nL408:        of employment, and both have strengths and limitations. The establishment survey\nL409:        employment series has a smaller margin of error on the measurement of month-to-\nL410:        month change than the household survey because of its much larger sample size. An\nL411:        over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in\nL412:        the establishment survey, while the threshold for a statistically significant change\nL413:        in the household survey is about 650,000. However, the household survey has a more\nL414:        expansive scope than the establishment survey because it includes self-employed\nL415:        workers whose businesses are unincorporated, unpaid family workers, agricultural\nL416:        workers, and private household workers, who are excluded by the establishment survey.\nL417:        The household survey also provides estimates of employment for demographic groups.\nL418:        For more information on the differences between the two surveys, please visit\nL419:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/ces_cps_trends.htm.\nL420:     2. Are undocumented immigrants counted in the surveys?\nL421: \nL422:        It is likely that both surveys include at least some undocumented immigrants. However,\nL423:        neither the establishment nor the household survey is designed to identify the legal\nL424:        status of workers. Therefore, it is not possible to determine how many are counted in\nL425:        either survey. The establishment survey does not collect data on the legal status of\nL426:        workers. The household survey does include questions which identify the foreign and\nL427:        native born, but it does not include questions about the legal status of the foreign\nL428:        born. Data on the foreign and native born are published each month in table A-7 of\nL429:        The Employment Situation news release.\nL430: \nL431:     3. Why does the establishment survey have revisions?\nL432: \nL433:        The establishment survey revises published estimates to improve its data series by\nL434:        incorporating additional information that was not available at the time of the\nL435:        initial publication of the estimates. The establishment survey revises its initial\nL436:        monthly estimates twice, in the immediately succeeding 2 months, to incorporate\nL437:        additional sample receipts from respondents in the survey and recalculated seasonal\nL438:        adjustment factors. For more information on the monthly revisions, please visit\nL439:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cestn.htm#Revisions-Between-Preliminary-and-Final-Data.\nL440: \nL441:        On an annual basis, the establishment survey incorporates a benchmark revision that\nL442:        re-anchors estimates to nearly complete employment counts available from unemployment\nL443:        insurance tax records. The benchmark helps to control for sampling and modeling errors\nL444:        in the estimates. For more information on the annual benchmark revision, please visit\nL445:        www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbmart.htm.\nL446:     4. Does the establishment survey sample include small firms?\nL447: \nL448:        Yes. About 46 percent of the establishment survey sample is comprised of business\nL449:        establishments with fewer than 20 employees. The establishment survey sample is\nL450:        designed to maximize the reliability of the statewide total nonfarm employment\nL451:        estimate; firms from all states, size classes, and industries are appropriately\nL452:        sampled to achieve that goal.\nL453:     5. Does the establishment survey account for employment from new businesses?\nL454: \nL455:        Yes. Monthly establishment survey estimates include an adjustment to account for\nL456:        the net employment change generated by business births and deaths. The adjustment\nL457:        comes from an econometric model that forecasts the monthly net jobs impact of\nL458:        business births and deaths based on the actual past values of the net impact that\nL459:        can be observed with a lag from the Quarterly Census of Employment and Wages. The\nL460:        establishment survey uses modeling rather than sampling for this purpose because\n"
